---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro'
import Exhibit from '../../components/Exhibit.astro'
import SpotDecision from '../../exhibits/SpotDecision'
import NameThePass from '../../exhibits/NameThePass'
import CountTheFusions from '../../exhibits/CountTheFusions'
import FusionXray from '../../exhibits/FusionXray'
import TimelineXray from '../../exhibits/TimelineXray'
---

<Base
  title="The reading gym · the XLA floor · Rudrite Kernels"
  description="The XLA path's stations: spot the compiler's decisions in real dumps, read fusion before/after pairs from a TPU, and read a real profile op by op."
>
  <div class="ws-ground">
    <section class="wband">
      <div class="wrap wgrid">
        <span class="wtag">the gym<br />xla floor</span>
        <div>
          <p class="mono-label serves">serves xla-path chapters 04 · 05 · 08 · 10 · streaks check themselves off; the same skill counts on every floor that drills it · <a class="wing-link" href="/gym">all floors</a></p>
          <h1 class="wh1">The compiler's decisions, drilled.</h1>
          <p class="wlede">
            The xla path teaches you to read what the compiler did and why. These stations hold
            the evidence: decisions the framework made silently, fusion plans captured from a real
            TPU compile, and a hardware profile with an op nobody in the program wrote. Everything
            here is generated or captured, never retyped.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="wband" id="drills">
      <div class="wrap wgrid">
        <span class="wtag">drills</span>
        <div>
          <h2 class="wh2">Active recall, one tap</h2>
          <div class="drill-wide">
            <Exhibit designator="GYM·03" title="spot the decision" provenance="find the line the framework wrote for you: upcasts, hidden broadcasts, remat marks · targets computed by scanning the real dumps · assigned in xla chapter 04">
              <SpotDecision client:visible />
            </Exhibit>
          </div>
          <div class="drill-wide">
            <Exhibit designator="GYM·12" title="which pipeline filed this pass?" provenance="17 names read out of two real dumps: this course's CPU compile and a Colab v6e · the shared names are the interesting ones · serves chapters 04 and 05">
              <NameThePass client:visible />
            </Exhibit>
          </div>
          <div class="drill-wide">
            <Exhibit designator="GYM·13" title="count the fusions" provenance="eight programs compiled and counted on jax 0.4.38 CPU · the reveal shows what each fusion actually holds · serves chapter 05">
              <CountTheFusions client:visible />
            </Exhibit>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="wband" id="fusion">
      <div class="wrap wgrid">
        <span class="wtag">before /<br />after</span>
        <div>
          <h2 class="wh2">Fusion, side by side</h2>
          <p class="wlede narrow">
            The StableHLO XLA received on the left, the optimized HLO it decided on the right,
            captured on a real TPU. Fusion ops light up in copper, and the lines still carrying
            each program's big intermediate are underlined: in the attention program that is the
            spill, sitting in the compiler's own plan. Chapter 05 teaches the policy that drew
            these boundaries.
          </p>
          <Exhibit designator="GYM·07" title="the fusion x-ray" provenance="three programs captured by labs/capture-hlo.ipynb on v6e-1 · ops verbatim, frame tables trimmed, runtime paths scrubbed">
            <FusionXray client:visible />
          </Exhibit>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="wband" id="timeline">
      <div class="wrap wgrid">
        <span class="wtag">the machine's<br />own account</span>
        <div>
          <h2 class="wh2">A real profile, read op by op</h2>
          <p class="wlede narrow">
            Twenty iterations of naive attention, traced on a v6e-1 and aggregated from the device
            plane: hardware time, not a cost model. Two things to find. The envelope equals the two
            compute ops alone, so the 89.6 µs copy hid entirely behind compute. And the
            second-largest op is a custom call nobody wrote: XLA:TPU pattern-matched the softmax
            and dispatched its own online-softmax kernel, exactly the escape hatch chapter 09
            teaches and the measurement habit chapter 10 closes on.
          </p>
          <Exhibit designator="GYM·08" title="the timeline x-ray" provenance="captured by labs/profile-bytes.ipynb on v6e-1, jax 0.11 · durations from the device plane, verbatim">
            <TimelineXray client:visible />
          </Exhibit>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .serves { margin-bottom: 1rem; letter-spacing: 0.04em; }
  .wing-link { color: var(--accent); text-decoration: none; }
  .wing-link:hover { text-decoration: underline; }
  .wh1 + .wlede { margin-top: 1rem; }
  .wlede.narrow { margin-bottom: 1.25rem; }
  h2.wh2 { margin-bottom: 1rem; }
  .drill-wide { margin-top: 1.25rem; }
</style>
